1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,732
In Part Zero, we proved that zero plus n equals n.

2
00:00:04,176 --> 00:00:09,581
We wrote the same claim in ACL2, with a hypothesis saying that n is
a natural number.

3
00:00:10,025 --> 00:00:17,987
Today, keep that one small example in view: the written claim, its
proof, and what it means.

4
00:00:18,431 --> 00:00:23,361
Those are three different things, and separating them explains the
purpose of this project.

5
00:00:23,806 --> 00:00:29,530
A proof can be checked by a computer, while questions remain about
the rules that computer is checking.

6
00:00:29,975 --> 00:00:36,161
We will build from our arithmetic example to models, consistency,
and Gödel's second incompleteness theorem.

7
00:00:36,606 --> 00:00:41,560
Then we can say precisely what is gained by moving an argument to a
weaker foundation.

8
00:00:42,004 --> 00:00:47,670
This is Part One of Landau Windows ACL Two: proofs, models, and
stronger foundations.

9
00:00:49,733 --> 00:00:53,810
A formal proof is a finite sequence or tree of justified steps.

10
00:00:54,255 --> 00:00:59,837
Some steps are starting axioms; others follow from earlier steps by
a stated inference rule.

11
00:01:00,281 --> 00:01:07,828
Here is one familiar rule: p holds, and p implies q, so q holds.

12
00:01:08,273 --> 00:01:13,001
The letters here name claims with truth values, rather than natural-
number inputs.

13
00:01:13,446 --> 00:01:16,847
We can inspect the four possible pairs of truth values.

14
00:01:17,292 --> 00:01:24,071
When p is true and q is false, the implication is false, so the two
premises cannot both hold.

15
00:01:24,516 --> 00:01:28,356
The only row in which both premises hold has q true as well.

16
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:31,313
That is why this inference preserves truth.

17
00:01:31,757 --> 00:01:36,996
The ACL2 statement underneath expresses the same rule, and our
teaching book checks it.

18
00:01:37,440 --> 00:01:42,809
A larger proof links many such justified steps; its final line is
the conclusion.

19
00:01:43,253 --> 00:01:47,153
Checking one supplied proof asks whether its steps obey the rules.

20
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:52,061
Now give the written symbols an interpretation.

21
00:01:52,515 --> 00:01:59,461
For ordinary arithmetic, zero names zero, successor adds one, and
the variables range over natural numbers.

22
00:01:59,915 --> 00:02:04,679
An interpretation that makes every axiom true is called a model of
the theory.

23
00:02:05,133 --> 00:02:10,733
To see why every axiom matters, try a different interpretation: a
clock with three positions.

24
00:02:11,188 --> 00:02:18,685
Its successor operation moves from zero to one, from one to two, and
from two back to zero.

25
00:02:19,139 --> 00:02:24,339
Before we call that clock operation by name, here is its full ACL2
definition.

26
00:02:24,794 --> 00:02:29,064
Defun introduces video-clock-next, with the input n.

27
00:02:29,518 --> 00:02:35,727
Nfix keeps n unchanged when it is natural, and returns zero for
other objects.

28
00:02:36,182 --> 00:02:41,203
We add one, then mod takes the remainder after division by three.

29
00:02:41,658 --> 00:02:48,064
For input two, the intermediate total is three, and the remainder is
zero.

30
00:02:48,518 --> 00:02:53,561
Now fold the definition into its name, while keeping the inputs and
outputs visible.

31
00:02:54,015 --> 00:02:58,112
This call is video-clock-next at two, and its value is zero.

32
00:02:58,567 --> 00:03:02,821
But PA requires that a successor never equals zero.

33
00:03:03,276 --> 00:03:07,082
The ordinary natural-number successor satisfies that rule.

34
00:03:07,536 --> 00:03:10,833
The clock fails it: the successor of two is zero.

35
00:03:11,288 --> 00:03:17,203
This proposed clock interpretation already fails one of the PA
successor axioms.

36
00:03:17,658 --> 00:03:22,894
A few familiar equations can survive an interpretation while another
axiom fails.

37
00:03:23,348 --> 00:03:29,373
To claim that we have a model, we must justify all the axioms,
including every instance of induction.

38
00:03:31,433 --> 00:03:35,700
Consistency concerns the entire collection of possible formal
proofs.

39
00:03:36,144 --> 00:03:42,722
For PA, we can express it by saying that no valid PA proof ends with
zero equals one.

40
00:03:43,167 --> 00:03:47,137
A system that derives a claim and its negation is inconsistent.

41
00:03:47,581 --> 00:03:55,238
In classical logic, contradictory premises allow any conclusion; the
ACL2 formula illustrates that principle.

42
00:03:55,682 --> 00:04:00,020
This is why consistency is a basic requirement for a useful
foundation.

43
00:04:00,464 --> 00:04:05,051
Checking this one arithmetic proof does not examine all other
possible proofs.

44
00:04:05,496 --> 00:04:10,888
Nor does checking a billion proof files establish that no longer
contradiction proof exists.

45
00:04:11,333 --> 00:04:15,813
We need a general argument about every permitted proof, however long
it is.

46
00:04:16,257 --> 00:04:18,781
One route to that argument uses a model.

47
00:04:20,833 --> 00:04:24,424
ZFC is a theory whose variables range over sets.

48
00:04:24,869 --> 00:04:29,645
Its name abbreviates the Zermelo Fraenkel axioms together with the
axiom of choice.

49
00:04:30,090 --> 00:04:33,776
We will use one construction rather than list all of its axioms.

50
00:04:34,220 --> 00:04:38,893
Start with the empty set and call it zero; it has no elements.

51
00:04:39,337 --> 00:04:46,584
To build the next number, collect the elements already present and
add the previous number itself.

52
00:04:47,029 --> 00:04:51,687
One is the set containing zero; two is the set containing zero and
one.

53
00:04:52,131 --> 00:04:56,528
Continuing gives a set representation of every ordinary natural
number.

54
00:04:56,973 --> 00:05:02,709
ZFC can also form the infinite collection of these natural numbers,
usually called omega.

55
00:05:03,153 --> 00:05:07,301
Within set theory, that whole collection is an object we can study.

56
00:05:07,746 --> 00:05:12,119
Addition and multiplication can be defined on it by their recursive
rules.

57
00:05:12,564 --> 00:05:18,288
Two other set operations will be useful: a union collects the
elements of a family of sets.

58
00:05:18,733 --> 00:05:24,599
A power set is the collection of all subsets of a given set; a two-
element set has four subsets.

59
00:05:25,044 --> 00:05:32,451
These are set-theoretic constructions; our ACL2 arithmetic examples
retain their explicit natural-number domains.

60
00:05:34,500 --> 00:05:40,959
Inside ZFC, construct the natural numbers and prove that they
satisfy the axioms of PA.

61
00:05:41,404 --> 00:05:48,207
This includes a uniform justification of the induction scheme,
rather than checking a finite sample of instances.

62
00:05:48,651 --> 00:05:53,439
Now take any alleged PA proof and follow its steps in that model.

63
00:05:53,884 --> 00:05:57,901
The axioms are true there, and the inference rules preserve truth.

64
00:05:58,346 --> 00:06:03,027
Induction on the finite proof therefore shows that its conclusion is
true in the model.

65
00:06:03,472 --> 00:06:07,359
This connection between derivations and truth is called soundness.

66
00:06:07,804 --> 00:06:15,365
Zero and one are distinct in the constructed number system, so a
valid PA proof cannot conclude that they are equal.

67
00:06:15,810 --> 00:06:19,875
That yields a ZFC proof of the consistency of PA.

68
00:06:21,933 --> 00:06:26,639
There is another useful viewpoint: a proof file is a finite string
of symbols.

69
00:06:27,083 --> 00:06:32,464
Assign numeric codes to symbols and encode finite lists of those
codes as natural numbers.

70
00:06:32,908 --> 00:06:38,028
Here is a tiny example using decimal digits, just to make the
mechanism visible.

71
00:06:38,473 --> 00:06:45,439
Start with a leading one, then append the digits two, zero, and
three.

72
00:06:45,883 --> 00:06:52,656
The successive codes are twelve, one hundred twenty, and one
thousand two hundred three.

73
00:06:53,101 --> 00:06:58,055
Each append operation multiplies the previous code by ten and adds
the new digit.

74
00:06:58,499 --> 00:07:02,636
The leading marker keeps a zero in the data from being lost at the
beginning.

75
00:07:03,080 --> 00:07:07,824
Now let us define that encoder before using its function name.

76
00:07:09,867 --> 00:07:15,515
The encoder processes a list of digits; let us first name the basic
list operations.

77
00:07:15,970 --> 00:07:21,003
Car selects the first item; cdr selects the remaining list.

78
00:07:21,458 --> 00:07:25,252
Consp asks whether there is a list cell still to process.

79
00:07:25,706 --> 00:07:29,112
Here is the complete definition of the digit-list predicate.

80
00:07:29,567 --> 00:07:38,130
For a nonempty list, the first item must be natural and below ten,
and the remaining list must pass the same test.

81
00:07:38,585 --> 00:07:46,276
At the end of a proper list, the tail is nil, the empty list; other
atomic tails are rejected.

82
00:07:46,730 --> 00:07:50,779
We can now fold this definition into the name video-digit-listp.

83
00:07:51,233 --> 00:07:54,082
Next, here is the actual recursive encoder.

84
00:07:54,536 --> 00:08:00,445
It has two inputs: the remaining digits and the accumulated code.

85
00:08:00,900 --> 00:08:09,948
If a digit remains, append the first item by multiplying the code by
ten and adding that digit, then recurse on the rest.

86
00:08:10,403 --> 00:08:13,967
When no list cell remains, return the accumulated code.

87
00:08:14,421 --> 00:08:19,185
The complete definition can now fold into the compact name video-
encode-digits.

88
00:08:19,639 --> 00:08:24,115
The quote marks this list as data; the starting code is one.

89
00:08:24,570 --> 00:08:30,291
Calling the defined function on two, zero, and three returns one
thousand two hundred three.

90
00:08:30,745 --> 00:08:37,255
For a digit list and a natural starting code, our checked ACL2
theorem proves that the output is natural.

91
00:08:37,709 --> 00:08:43,370
A real encoding of formulas and proofs also needs a specified
grammar and proof rules.

92
00:08:43,824 --> 00:08:51,170
The definitions we have shown implement the toy encoder; a complete
PA proof checker is a separate construction.

93
00:08:51,624 --> 00:08:57,915
With a full encoding and checker in place, questions about proofs
can be expressed as questions about numbers.

94
00:08:59,967 --> 00:09:04,850
We can now unpack the phrase PA is consistent in arithmetic
language.

95
00:09:05,294 --> 00:09:12,370
Let a proof predicate test whether a natural-number code describes a
valid PA proof of the chosen conclusion.

96
00:09:12,814 --> 00:09:17,365
For consistency, the chosen conclusion is the contradiction zero
equals one.

97
00:09:17,810 --> 00:09:26,361
The claim is: for every natural-number code p, that code does not
describe a PA contradiction proof.

98
00:09:26,805 --> 00:09:32,530
The notation Con of PA is a short name for this standard formal
consistency statement.

99
00:09:32,974 --> 00:09:39,934
To turn this into a concrete ACL2 statement, we would first need a
full PA proof checker.

100
00:09:40,379 --> 00:09:47,149
That full checker is not implemented in the teaching book; this is a
specification of what it would have to decide.

101
00:09:47,593 --> 00:09:52,903
We are displaying a specification, not a consistency theorem
admitted in our teaching book.

102
00:09:53,347 --> 00:10:01,253
Notice the jump in scope: the digit example computed one code, while
consistency rules out every contradiction-proof code.

103
00:10:01,697 --> 00:10:04,838
That universal claim is where Gödel's theorem enters.

104
00:10:06,900 --> 00:10:13,727
Gödel's second incompleteness theorem applies to theories with
enough arithmetic and an effective description of their axioms.

105
00:10:14,171 --> 00:10:17,217
PA is one of the standard examples.

106
00:10:17,661 --> 00:10:23,564
Effective means that the rules can be specified by algorithms, as
they are for the theories discussed here.

107
00:10:24,008 --> 00:10:29,318
Use the usual arithmetic encoding of formal proofs and the resulting
consistency statement.

108
00:10:29,762 --> 00:10:35,976
If such a theory is consistent, it cannot prove that standard
statement of its own consistency.

109
00:10:36,420 --> 00:10:41,753
We are stating the theorem here; its proof requires the formal
machinery behind provability.

110
00:10:42,198 --> 00:10:48,918
For PA, the forbidden self-proof would be a PA derivation of Con of
PA.

111
00:10:49,362 --> 00:10:55,454
For ZFC, the analogous limitation concerns a ZFC derivation of Con
of ZFC.

112
00:10:55,899 --> 00:11:02,607
This is a precise result with hypotheses, rather than a blanket
slogan about every conceivable formal system.

113
00:11:03,051 --> 00:11:07,259
It leaves room for a stronger theory to prove the consistency of a
weaker one.

114
00:11:07,703 --> 00:11:13,724
The ZFC argument about the natural-number model of PA is exactly
that kind of result.

115
00:11:15,767 --> 00:11:23,814
Compare the two statements on screen: ZFC proves Con of PA, and ZFC
proves Con of ZFC.

116
00:11:24,259 --> 00:11:27,127
The model construction supplies the first statement.

117
00:11:27,571 --> 00:11:34,528
If ZFC is consistent, Gödel's second theorem rules out the second
statement, with its standard proof encoding.

118
00:11:34,973 --> 00:11:39,619
A consistency argument must therefore say which background supplies
its reasoning.

119
00:11:40,063 --> 00:11:45,290
Likewise, a proof of an ordinary arithmetic claim comes with some
set of dependencies.

120
00:11:45,734 --> 00:11:51,743
The claim zero plus n equals n can be established without building
the whole set-theoretic model first.

121
00:11:52,187 --> 00:11:58,125
A successful reduction replaces a use of stronger resources with a
derivation using weaker ones.

122
00:11:58,570 --> 00:12:04,140
The mathematical conclusion stays the same, while the assumptions we
rely on become smaller.

123
00:12:04,584 --> 00:12:09,716
This is the sense in which a proof with fewer foundational
assumptions is a stronger assurance.

124
00:12:10,161 --> 00:12:16,525
Finding such a reduction is mathematical work; writing the statement
in arithmetic is only the starting point.

125
00:12:18,567 --> 00:12:24,161
The model idea also explains why inaccessible cardinals enter
discussions of foundations.

126
00:12:24,605 --> 00:12:30,353
A cardinal measures the size of a set: equal sizes mean that the
elements can be paired one for one.

127
00:12:30,798 --> 00:12:33,524
Infinite sets also have cardinal sizes.

128
00:12:33,968 --> 00:12:40,368
A strongly inaccessible cardinal, called kappa, is an uncountable
size with two closure properties.

129
00:12:40,813 --> 00:12:45,636
First, the power set of any set smaller than kappa is still smaller
than kappa.

130
00:12:46,081 --> 00:12:51,888
Second, a union of fewer than kappa sets, each smaller than kappa,
is still smaller than kappa.

131
00:12:52,333 --> 00:12:56,682
These conditions let us build a particularly well-behaved universe
of sets.

132
00:12:57,127 --> 00:13:03,550
Build sets in stages, taking power sets at successor stages and
collecting earlier stages at limit stages.

133
00:13:03,995 --> 00:13:08,190
The sets appearing before stage kappa form the collection written V
kappa.

134
00:13:08,635 --> 00:13:13,186
If kappa is strongly inaccessible, V kappa is a model of ZFC.

135
00:13:13,630 --> 00:13:19,663
Consequently, ZFC with that extra existence assumption proves the
consistency of ZFC.

136
00:13:20,107 --> 00:13:26,164
If ZFC is consistent, it therefore cannot prove that such an
inaccessible cardinal exists.

137
00:13:26,608 --> 00:13:33,435
A standard set-theoretic treatment of Lean's universe hierarchy uses
a sequence of suitable inaccessible sizes.

138
00:13:33,879 --> 00:13:40,955
That describes a way to model the foundation; the dependencies of a
particular theorem still need their own examination.

139
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:47,729
Return to our actual goal: an arithmetic account of the windowed
Landau argument.

140
00:13:48,173 --> 00:13:54,087
We want the same intended conclusion, reached through explicit
functions and checked theorems about their behavior.

141
00:13:54,532 --> 00:14:00,801
For each component, we need a statement, a proof, and an
interpretation connecting it to the mathematics.

142
00:14:01,246 --> 00:14:05,596
An ACL2 theorem is a concrete artifact in ACL2's logic.

143
00:14:06,040 --> 00:14:13,127
A claim about PA strength additionally needs the arithmetic
interpretation of the fragment and principles used.

144
00:14:13,572 --> 00:14:17,862
The programming language alone does not settle that foundational
comparison.

145
00:14:18,307 --> 00:14:24,007
Nor does the existence of one verified numerical example establish a
general success theorem.

146
00:14:24,452 --> 00:14:30,733
The later parts will keep the mathematical statement beside its ACL2
counterpart and state the input domains.

147
00:14:31,178 --> 00:14:37,163
They will also distinguish a proved component from an outstanding
interpretation or composition obligation.

148
00:14:37,607 --> 00:14:41,791
That is how reducing assumptions becomes a precise, reviewable
result.

149
00:14:43,833 --> 00:14:50,980
We now have three separate questions: what follows from the rules,
what the symbols mean, and what justifies the rules.

150
00:14:51,424 --> 00:14:57,848
A model connects the first two, and a consistency proof names the
background in which that connection is justified.

151
00:14:58,293 --> 00:15:01,362
Gödel's theorem explains why the background matters.

152
00:15:01,807 --> 00:15:05,599
The next part returns to calculations we can inspect directly.

153
00:15:06,044 --> 00:15:12,681
We will represent fractions and intervals exactly, then prove that
their bounds survive arithmetic operations.

154
00:15:13,125 --> 00:15:20,947
For example, two thirds lies between one half and three quarters;
ACL2 can express that comparison with exact rationals.

155
00:15:21,392 --> 00:15:26,808
Those small guarantees will become the error budgets behind the
later search and Landau arguments.

156
00:15:27,253 --> 00:15:30,927
Same mathematical target, with the dependencies made explicit.
